    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Gymnasts who report spending more time training seem to be protected from pathological eating disturbances and that high levels of self esteem may serve as a more direct protective mechanism."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The sample size was relatively small. It is not feasible to determine why 14% elected not to participate, and it is possible that they may have differed from those who participated in the research. The self-report nature nature of the study may have been problematic in that it was difficult to assess for accuracy and honesty in these measures. The cross sectional design does not allow for an actual test of the dynamic process between the study variables across time and is therefore limited in what it can conclude about actual risk and protective factors. While the onset of puberty is typically associated with a significant weight gain for Females, it may be that, due to training regimens, gymnasts in the current study had not gained a considerable amount of weight, even though they reported experiencing accompanying development changes such as breast developmale t and growth of body hair."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future studies would benefit from the addition of a procedure that analyzes body fat or using a longitudinal design to assess for weight gain during puberty. Continued exploration of this population is vital in order ton increase understanding of these and additional factors that may predispose or protect gymnasts from body dissatisfaction and pathological eating disturbances."
    },
